---
/**
 * XEmbeds — the one place this site loads something from X, and the only page
 * that renders it is `/library/kind/post`.
 *
 * It draws nothing. What it ships is a script that stamps the site's theme onto
 * every `blockquote.twitter-tweet` on the page, fetches X's widget factory,
 * which swaps each of those blockquotes for the real embed, and then watches
 * for those embeds to actually stand up before letting the fallback inside each
 * blockquote go. The last part is the one worth reading: see the note above
 * `HYDRATE`.
 *
 * ## Why this is a component and not four lines in `PostWall.astro`
 *
 * **The host is written once, here, and nowhere else in the build.** That is
 * the arrangement `VideoFacade.astro` keeps for YouTube and for the same
 * reason: `scripts/validate-schema.mjs` reads `dist/` and fails if a Twitter
 * host appears on any page that has not declared an embed — or fails to appear
 * on one that has — so "which pages talk to X" is a property of the built HTML
 * rather than of anyone's memory. A page that starts loading this has to edit
 * /privacy in the same commit, and the guard is what makes that a build failure
 * instead of a note.
 *
 * ## What this costs, stated rather than discovered
 *
 * Everything else on this site is served from this origin. This is not: the
 * posts page fetches `widgets.js` from X on load, without being pressed, and
 * every card it renders becomes an iframe of theirs. X therefore sees the IP of
 * anyone who opens that page, and whatever happens inside their frame is theirs
 * rather than ours. `data-dnt="true"` on every blockquote is the one thing we
 * can ask for — it tells X not to use the visit for tailoring — and it is a
 * request rather than a guarantee. /privacy says all of that in his own words.
 *
 * **It is Aayush's call, made with the trade in front of him.** The static
 * cards this replaces fetched nothing and looked, in his words, kind of odd: a
 * hand-built imitation of a tweet is uncanny in the way an imitation of
 * anything familiar is. The page each card points at is the compensation —
 * `/library/<slug>` renders the post's own words from files in this repository
 * and loads nothing from anyone — so the wall is the only place on the site
 * where reading costs a request to X, and the page for reading properly never
 * does.
 *
 * ## The theme, and the flip we do not chase
 *
 * X's embed takes its theme at creation and an iframe on another origin cannot
 * be restyled afterwards. So the script resolves the theme once, before the
 * factory arrives, and stamps `data-theme="dark"` on the blockquotes when it is
 * dark. Three-state, so it reads the same attribute on `<html>` everything else
 * reads and falls through to the OS query when nothing is pinned.
 *
 * **A flip after that point is accepted rather than handled, and the cost is
 * the reasoning.** Re-theming would mean tearing down every iframe on the page
 * and asking X to build twenty-four new ones: a second full round of
 * third-party requests, a visible flash of the whole wall, and the reader's
 * place in it gone — all to correct the one page on the site where a theme
 * press is not instant. So the wall keeps the theme it loaded with until the
 * next navigation, which is one press away on any tab above it. The page around
 * it flips normally; it is the embeds that lag.
 *
 * ## Why the script is a string
 *
 * `is:inline`, built here and emitted with `set:html`, which is the shape
 * `lib/theme.ts › PREPAINT` already has. A bundled `<script>` would be lifted
 * into a hashed module under `/_astro/`, and then the widget host would not be
 * in any page's HTML at all — which would leave the dist guard above with
 * nothing to check on the page that is actually making the request. Inline, the
 * host sits in the one document that loads it, and the check is per-page and
 * exact. The price is that this body is a string rather than typed source, so
 * the two values it must not get wrong — the attribute name and the host — are
 * interpolated from constants instead of typed out.
 */
import { ATTRIBUTE } from "../lib/theme";

/**
 * The widget factory, and the only URL in this build that is neither on this
 * domain nor logo.dev.
 *
 * `platform.twitter.com` rather than an `x.com` spelling because that is the
 * host X's own embed documentation names and serves the factory from. The
 * permalink inside each blockquote is x.com, because that is where the post
 * actually is.
 */
const WIDGETS = "https://platform.twitter.com/widgets.js";

/**
 * How tall a rendered widget has to be before the fallback under it stands
 * down, and how long we keep asking.
 *
 * X's shortest real card is a couple of hundred pixels; a container they have
 * inserted but not yet filled is exactly zero. Forty is nowhere near either, so
 * it is a threshold rather than a measurement, and it is written as a number
 * because there is nothing to derive it from.
 *
 * Sixty steps of 250ms is fifteen seconds, which is the ceiling and not the
 * expected cost: the loop stops the moment every embed is up, and on a page
 * where X never answers it stops on its own with every fallback still on
 * screen — which is the outcome this whole mechanism exists to guarantee.
 */
const MIN_EMBED = 40;
const STEP_MS = 250;
const MAX_STEPS = 60;

/**
 * Stamp the theme, fetch the factory, then watch for the embeds to actually
 * arrive — and do none of it when the page turned out to have no embeds on it.
 *
 * The empty case is not defensive tidiness: `PostWall.astro` renders no cards
 * at all when every post is one the pipeline could not read, and a page showing
 * no embeds must not go and ask X for the machinery to show them.
 *
 * The factory is appended rather than written into the markup as a static
 * `<script async src>`. With a static tag it can run before this does — `async`
 * carries no ordering — and any blockquote it reached first would be built in
 * the light theme and stay there. Appending it after the attributes are on is
 * the only version with a defined order.
 *
 * ## The watch, and why a class was not enough
 *
 * `TweetCard.astro` used to hide its fallback the moment X's container appeared
 * beside it. Their container appears long before their iframe has a height, so
 * the wall spent the whole load — and, on a load where X never sizes anything,
 * the rest of the visit — showing `notes` bars with nothing above them.
 * Measured on the shipped page: twenty-four widgets at zero height, the
 * document down from 6311px to 1835px.
 *
 * So a class is not evidence and a height is. This marks each blockquote
 * `data-embedded` only while the element X put in front of it is really
 * standing up, and unmarks it if it is not, so every failure lands on the
 * fallback rather than on an empty column. It costs one `getBoundingClientRect`
 * per card per step, for as few steps as the page needs.
 */
const HYDRATE = `(function(){
var q=document.querySelectorAll("blockquote.twitter-tweet");
if(!q.length)return;
var p=document.documentElement.getAttribute(${JSON.stringify(ATTRIBUTE)});
var dark=p==="dark"||(p!=="light"&&window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches);
if(dark)for(var i=0;i<q.length;i++)q[i].setAttribute("data-theme","dark");
var s=document.createElement("script");
s.src=${JSON.stringify(WIDGETS)};
s.async=true;
document.head.appendChild(s);
var n=0;
var t=setInterval(function(){
var pending=0;
for(var j=0;j<q.length;j++){
var w=q[j].previousElementSibling;
if(w&&w.getBoundingClientRect().height>${MIN_EMBED})q[j].setAttribute("data-embedded","");
else{q[j].removeAttribute("data-embedded");pending++;}
}
if(!pending||++n>${MAX_STEPS})clearInterval(t);
},${STEP_MS});
})();`;
---

<script is:inline set:html={HYDRATE} />
